    <!-- 4. Can a Screenshot Checker Detect Fake Screenshots? -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        Can a Screenshot Checker Detect Fake Screenshots?
      </h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>fake screenshot checker</strong> can identify signals that may indicate manipulation, but no automated software can guarantee that every screenshot is authentic or counterfeit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Edited screenshots can leave clues. Some you can spot by eye; some a tool can surface:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 text-sm pl-1">
        <li><strong>By eye: fonts, spacing and alignment.</strong> Zoom in on amounts, names and times and compare them with the text around them. ScreenshotChecker does not measure fonts or layout, so this step is yours.</li>
        <li><strong>Text that does not add up:</strong> OCR reads the words, and rules flag amounts, dates, references or wording that contradict each other or match common scam patterns.</li>
        <li><strong>Localized Compression Inconsistencies:</strong> Modifying a portion of an image and resaving can produce varying JPEG compression rates between modified and original regions.</li>
        <li><strong>Contradictory Metadata:</strong> Internal EXIF tags or editor software fingerprints may contradict visible timestamps.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink font-normal pt-2">Why Screenshot Forensics Differs from Photo Forensics</h3>
      <p>
        Checking a screenshot like a camera photo leads to the wrong answer. A photo usually records the camera make and model, exposure settings and sometimes a GPS location, and its pixels carry the noise pattern of a camera sensor. A screenshot is copied straight from the screen: it has no lens, no sensor noise and, by design, little or no camera metadata. Most phones save screenshots as PNG files, and chat apps often re-save them as compressed JPEGs when they are forwarded.
      </p>
      <p>
        So missing EXIF data is expected on a screenshot and proves nothing on its own, and a screenshot forwarded through a chat app has been recompressed all over, which can hide the traces of an edit. For screenshots, the text, the layout and the original source tell you more than the file does. For camera photos, the <a href="/blog/how-to-tell-if-a-photo-is-edited/" class="text-primary underline hover:text-primary-active">photo editing guide</a> covers the camera-specific checks.
      </p>
      <p>
        ScreenshotChecker does not claim to always tell if a screenshot is fake. Instead, it identifies signals that may indicate manipulation and helps you investigate the image further.
      </p>
    </section>

