    <!-- 5. Screenshot Verification: Why Context Matters -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        Screenshot Verification: Why Context Matters
      </h2>
      <p>
        When performing <strong>screenshot verification</strong>, remember that a screenshot is a visual representation of information rather than the original source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Screenshots are commonly shared as proof of bank transfers, WhatsApp chats, emails, delivery updates, and social media posts. However, visual interfaces can easily be simulated through browser developer tools, templates, or mockup software.
      </p>
      <p>
        For critical transactions, visual evidence alone is never conclusive. <strong>Always verify the transaction in your own bank or payment application rather than relying only on a screenshot.</strong>         A <strong>screenshot authenticity checker</strong> is a valuable inspection aid, but verifying the original source remains essential.
      </p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink font-normal pt-2">Can a Screenshot Be Used as Evidence?</h3>
      <p>
        Courts can accept screenshots, but they ask how you know the image is what you say it is. In the United States, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_901" class="text-primary underline hover:text-primary-active" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Federal Rule of Evidence 901</a> requires evidence sufficient to support a finding that an item is what its proponent claims, often testimony from someone who saw the messages, or details only the real sender would know. In India, a copy of an electronic record such as a screenshot or printout generally needs a certificate under Section 63 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, which replaced Section 65B of the Evidence Act from 1 July 2024; the certificate includes the record's hash value, a requirement the <a href="https://www.livelaw.in/top-stories/supreme-court-rejects-challenge-to-s634-bsa-mandating-hash-value-disclosure-for-electronic-evidence-535950" class="text-primary underline hover:text-primary-active" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Supreme Court upheld</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a conversation or payment might end up in a dispute, keep the original phone and file, export the full chat, and record the screen while scrolling through the conversation instead of relying on one screenshot. This is general information, not legal advice.
      </p>
    </section>

